                    <occupation>writer</occupation>
                    <occupation/>
                    <note type="editorial">
                        <p>Thomas Holcroft was one of Godwin's closest friends for many years. He
                            was a sigificant collaborator in Godwin's writings, notably
                                <title>Political Justice</title> as well as being an important
                            dramatist and novelist in his own right. Holcroft left for Germany in
                            1799, partly due to the notoriety he had gained as an 'acquitted felon'
                            of the 1794 Treason Trials. He returned to England in 1802 and had
                            further success on the stage. Godwin and he fell out over a character in
                            Godwin's <title>Fleetwood</title> which Holcroft felt was based on him.
                            They were reconciled on Holcroft's deathbed.</p>
                        <p>Godwin’s first mention of Holcroft is in 1788, which is used consistently
                            until the first instance of abbreviation of ‘Ht’ in 1792. Other
                            variations have included ‘H’ (where context has determined that the
                            initial refers to Holcroft) and Holct. 1795 is the last mention of
                            Holcroft’s full name, and thereafter Godwin uses Ht consistently. Where
                            possible, when Godwin refers to Holcrofts plural, we have established
                            his partner based on chronology, as he married several times. Holcroft
                            married in 1765 and has a daughter Ann. His wife died prematurely but no
                            further details are known. In 1774 he married Matilda Tipler, wiith whom
                            he had his son William and daughter Sophy, this wife dying c 1775. He
                            married his third wife, Dinah Robinson, in 1778, with whom he had a
                            daughter Fanny. The diary notes the separation of Holcroft from his
                            third wife on 6 January 1790, and notes her death on 31 October 1790.
                            Godwin seems to have had no independent relationship with Holcroft's
                            third wife (hence, left uncoded). He married his fourth wife, Louisa
                            Mercier on 4 March 1799. The <title>DNB</title> makes no mention of
                            issue from Hocroft's fourth marriage, but Godwin refers to L Ht and sons
                            in the diary from 1803. Also, the account of Holcroft's death in his
                                <title>Life</title> refers to an incident where Holcroft asks his
                            wife if it is her children making a noise on the stair (p. 310). The
                                <title>Morning Chronicle</title> (25 March 1809) reporting
                            Holcroft's death refers to him having left an amiable wife and six
                            children under the age of nine and two daughters from a previous
                            marriage! </p>
                        <p> There is a reference to a 'Ht' on 4 Jan 1802 which has been left uncoded
                            as Holcroft is still abroad. '2 Hts' and '3 Holcrofts' entries which
                            appear 1802-5 have been coded for Thomas and Louisa. After Holcroft
                            marries Louisa, all instances of 'Hts' have been coded for her as well.
                            Similarly to other entries, she has also been included in any meals
                            Godwin takes at 'Ht's' and for 'Call on Hts' but NOT for any 'call on
                            Ht' or 'Call at Ht's'. N.B. This is despite evidence of Godwin's
                            inconsistent use of the possessive apostrophe ie 'Dine with Nicholsons,
                            Smiths, Ht's, etc' Note also on 9 Aug 1804, Godwin refers to 'L and T
                            Ht' this has been coded as Thomas and Louisa, despite the unusual nature
                            of the reference.</p>
                        <p>There are also a number of occasions when Godwin uses H to refer to
                            Holcroft - many of these are clear, althopugh the occasions in 1801 and
                            1802 are suspect - since, although Holcroft may have made fleeting
                            returns to the UK during the peace of Amiens, we have no corroboration.
                            The H's in 1808 clearly refer to Hodgkins, Godwin's errant shopman. We
                            have not coded the Hs for 1812, 1819, 1820, 1823, and 1825unless we have
                            the right contextual data, and it is likely that these are oftn to be
                            identified from those with whom they appear. Nothing suggests they are references to a single person.</p>
                        <p> There is a range of references to Holcroft's activities that can be
                            traced through the entries, although the meaning of many of them remains
                            obscure. For example, 17 September 1788 'Explain to Holcroft' and 29
                            September 1788 'Holcroft explains to Robinson'. In November 1789 a
                            series of entries from 8 November 1789 to 17 November 1789 provides a
                            narrative of the running away of Holcroft's son, their pursuit to
                            Gravesend and then Deal; the suicide of his son, and then his funeral.
                            This is followed in 6 January 1790 by 'Separation of H and sa femme' .
                            Holcroft's third wife (Diana Robinson) dies on 31 October 1790 and an
                            inquest and funeral follow on 2-3 November 1790. Unsurprisingly,
                            Holcroft also features largely in references to quarrels or
                            'demêlé'.</p>
                        <p>The diary also refers to <persName key="HOL07">Harwood
                                Holcroft</persName>, probably one of his sons, named in gratitude
                            after <persName key="HAR01">Col. Harwood</persName> who was a close
                            friend to Holcroft as well as being his son-in-law (with Ann).</p>
                    </note>
